---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/analytics';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/analytics');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/analytics');
const candidates = ['sequenzy', 'klaviyo', 'drip', 'sendlane', 'omnisend', 'yotpo-email-sms', 'mailchimp', 'activecampaign', 'shopify-email', 'brevo'].map(s => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><article class="prose-shopify card mt-6">
<h1>Shopify SMS analytics: reconcile credit, cost and incremental value</h1>
<p>This playbook is for the retention operator and finance partner deciding whether to keep, change or stop an SMS flow. A campaign dashboard assigns credit according to rules. It does not show what the same customers would have bought without the text.</p>
<h2>Write a measurement contract</h2><p>Record the eligible population, assignment unit, reporting timezone, currency, purchase window and order definition before the campaign. Distinguish enrolled people, attempted sends, delivered messages and unique recipients. Revenue per delivered message and revenue per eligible customer answer different questions; label both denominators.</p><p>For every export retain campaign/flow ID, customer ID, message ID, event time, destination, delivery status, billed segments, fee and opt-out event. Join order lines using stable customer and order identifiers where available, not email alone. Handle guest identities, merged profiles and duplicate orders explicitly. Keep personal information access limited to the reporting job.</p>
<h2>Reconcile attribution before comparing platforms</h2><p><a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/reports-and-analytics/shopify-reports/report-types/default-reports/marketing-reports">Shopify documents several attribution models</a>. Last click and last non-direct click distribute credit differently; any-click can allocate more credit than the number of orders. Compare exports using a named model, matching dates and matching order scope. Do not add email and SMS dashboard revenue as though they were disjoint orders.</p><p>Maintain an exception bucket for unmatched orders and missing campaign tags. A rising mismatch rate can reflect broken links or changed consent/tracking behavior. A difference between dashboards is a reconciliation question, not immediate proof of fraud or campaign success.</p>
<h2>A hypothetical holdout calculation</h2><p>Suppose a proposed experiment randomly assigns 1,000 eligible customers to SMS and 1,000 to a holdout. If 80 and 60 respectively purchase during the same observation window, purchase rates are 8% and 6%, an observed difference of 2 percentage points. Applied to the 1,000 treated customers, that is an estimate of 20 additional orders. These are invented teaching numbers, not observed results or a claim of statistical certainty.</p><p>If the average contribution per additional order were $20 and the total incremental campaign cost were $50, the illustrative estimate would be 20 × $20 − $50 = $350. This simplification assumes comparable order margins. In a real experiment, compare contribution per assigned customer directly, incorporating discounts, returns, fulfillment and messaging cost without deducting the same cost twice.</p>
<h2>Design the experiment around the purchase cycle</h2><p>Randomize before sending and preserve assignment through retries. Choose sample size and duration from baseline purchase rate, the smallest effect worth acting on and available traffic; there is no universal holdout percentage or thirty-day rule. Prevent other SMS flows from replacing the withheld treatment. Record concurrent email offers and promotions in both groups. If the question is combined email plus SMS impact, withhold the combined treatment instead.</p><p>For subscriptions, distinguish scheduled renewal revenue from new demand and decide the treatment target before analysis. For wholesale, account-level assignment may avoid multiple buyers at one business receiving different treatments. Report uncertainty and delayed returns before scaling a small apparent lift.</p>
<h2>Turn reporting into a decision</h2><p>Use <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery</a> to investigate cancellation leakage, <a href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">discount strategy</a> to investigate margin erosion and <a href="/use-cases/segmentation">segmentation</a> to investigate audience mix. Review declining contribution with delivery errors, fee changes, stock availability and opt-outs before choosing a new subject line.</p><p>Log one action, its owner, expected effect and follow-up date. Preserve the export and definitions used to make the decision. A supplier demonstration should show raw export fields, refund handling and attribution settings; do not infer holdout capability from an attractive reporting screen.</p>
<h2>App discovery and demonstration brief</h2><p>The catalog below preserves routes for comparison; it is not a ranking or confirmation that each app supports this workflow or native SMS. For this use case, ask each supplier to demonstrate the state transitions described above, the required integration and plan, export fields, failure handling and the full messaging quote. Include Sequenzy in that evaluation only where its documented channel and integration scope meets your requirements.</p><ul>{candidates.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul><p>For permission and recipient timing design, use the <a href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours</a> guides. This workflow plan does not establish legal applicability or guarantee delivery.</p></article></section></BaseLayout>
